ldren":[{"type":"text","value":"In this chapter, we will consider general maneuvers that can change the orbit of a spacecraft. All orbital changes require application of an external force to modify one or more of the orbital elements. This external force most commonly comes from an on-board propulsion system, although it can also come from gravitational interactions with other celestial bodies (so-called slingshot maneuvers).","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"GJaLpmgiqU"}],"key":"dRw1jYJTZm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this chapter, we are going to focus on maneuvers using on-board propulsion systems. In this case, the force is called ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"m9r5A02D9t"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"thrust","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"i8aWrmHY1n"}],"key":"yOynN4b45i"},{"type":"text","value":". In general, we will not be concerned with the source of the thrust, except insofar as it determines the type of maneuver.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"fTjmYgxzvC"}],"key":"cYLBDPN0Ev"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will consider two types of maneuvers:","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"s8gbteSWY9"}],"key":"hp79fEnXjS"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Impulsive maneuvers","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"itVQU5DyzB"}],"key":"NQlD012LYO"}],"key":"JJ3JPR1Ukv"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-impulsive maneuvers","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"Uf6Q2yy6UY"}],"key":"zVtgoOx1c8"}],"key":"d2h378uIuB"}],"key":"lwdIrVXMZ8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the case of an ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"iyBbRW9YGU"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"impulsive maneuver","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"MtrfJSkWqZ"}],"key":"kjL5Y0Ou9R"},{"type":"text","value":", the application of the force is very short relative to the time scale of the coasting period of the maneuver. Therefore, we can assume that the velocity vector changes instantaneously, either magnitude, direction, or both. This also means that the position of the spacecraft does not change during the maneuver.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"xq4wksRuPB"}],"key":"EUGyeWt8H1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The advantage of this approach is that we can neglect the additional force term in the equations of motion and treat the transfer orbit exactly the same as we did in previous chapters. The disadvantage is that the magnitude of the thrust must be rather large to accomplish the required energy change in a short time interval.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"EPav0TeEGA"}],"key":"bHJAucZwHx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the case of a ","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"iAmuQYnmdl"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-impulsive maneuver","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"NQzTndBTjT"}],"key":"dOyG1U2dSp"},{"type":"text","value":", by contrast, the force is applied over a significant portion of the transfer. As a result, the magnitude of the thrust can be much lower to accomplish the same energy change. The disadvantage is that we must solve the equations of motion including the thrust term, resulting in a more complicated system of equations to solve.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"VPCkhdGV2B"}],"key":"RMJlHUUd2s"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change of Velocity","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"Ddthv0jjYJ"}],"identifier":"change-of-velocity","label":"Change of Velocity","html_id":"change-of-velocity","implicit":true,"key":"AFfy4IKR0Z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will now focus on impulsive maneuvers. For impulsive maneuvers, the relevant metric we are interested in is the change of velocity of the spacecraft, ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"ichXkJ0Aii"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\Delta \\vector{v}","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi